<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Lordship of Rimini</h1><p class="st-meta">1295 CE – 1500 CE · capital Rimini</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Malatesta lordship of Rimini, home of Sigismondo Malatesta's Tempio Malatestiano and of the tragedy of Paolo and Francesca.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malatesta-da-verucchio">Malatesta da Verucchio</a>, leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-welf">Guelphs</a> of Rimini, drove out the Ghibelline Parcitadi family in 1295 and made himself lord of the city. His heirs ruled Rimini as papal vicars and spread through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romagna">Romagna</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marche">Marche</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malatesta-family">Malatesta</a> were warlords and condottieri who governed Rimini, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cesena">Cesena</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fano">Fano</a> and other towns. Malatesta's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giovanni-malatesta">Gianciotto</a> killed his wife <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francesca-da-rimini">Francesca da Polenta</a> and his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paolo-malatesta">Paolo</a>, a story <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dante-alighieri">Dante</a> told in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inferno-dante">Inferno</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigismondo-pandolfo-malatesta">Sigismondo Pandolfo Malatesta</a>, a brilliant and ruthless captain, had <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leon-battista-alberti">Leon Battista Alberti</a> remodel the church of San Francesco as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tempio-malatestiano">Tempio Malatestiano</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-pius-ii">Pope Pius II</a> publicly condemned Sigismondo in 1462 and took most of his lands.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1500 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cesare-borgia">Cesare Borgia</a> drove out <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pandolfo-iv-malatesta">Pandolfo IV Malatesta</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> held Rimini from 1503 to 1509, after which it passed to direct papal rule in the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/papal-states">Papal States</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="other, 1295 CE – 1500 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M26 24 H74 V52 Q74 74 50 84 Q26 74 26 52 Z"/><path d="M50 24 V84 M26 46 H74"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">other</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Italy</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:85.47%;width:4.08%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1295 CE – 1500 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Rimini</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Malatesta da Verucchio, Sigismondo Pandolfo Malatesta, Pandolfo IV Malatesta</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Romagnol, Latin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Malatesta lordship, Rimini</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1398&amp;focus=lordship-of-rimini" aria-label="Lordship of Rimini on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4981 618 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Lordship of Rimini lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5301 874L5304 942"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5301 874h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5301 874h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Rimini. <a href="/atlas?year=1398&amp;focus=lordship-of-rimini">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

